## Refactoring for Reusability

To avoid duplicating code across your application, you can extract repetitive JSX and logic into separate, reusable components. In this tutorial, the logic for displaying a list of blogs was moved from the `Home` component into a new, dedicated `BlogList` component.

*   **Problem**: Displaying a blog list on multiple pages (Home, Search, Category) would require repeating the same mapping logic.
*   **Solution**: Create a single `BlogList.js` component that can be imported and used wherever it's needed.

## Understanding Props

**Props** (short for properties) are the mechanism for passing data from a parent component to a child component. This allows reusable child components to be dynamic and display different data based on where they are used.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Data flow in React is one-way: from parent to child. Child components receive props as read-only data and should not modify them directly.

## Passing and Receiving Props

### Passing Props (Parent Component)

In the parent component's JSX, you pass props to the child component as if they were HTML attributes.

*   **Passing dynamic data** (e.g., from state): Use curly braces `{}`.
    ```jsx
    <BlogList blogs={blogs} />
    ```
*   **Passing static data** (e.g., a string): Use double quotes `""`.
    ```jsx
    <BlogList title="All Blogs!" />
    ```

### Receiving Props (Child Component)

The child functional component automatically receives a `props` object as its first argument, which contains all the properties passed down from the parent.

```jsx
const BlogList = (props) => {
  const blogs = props.blogs;
  const title = props.title;

  return (
    <div className="blog-list">
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {blogs.map(blog => (
        <div className="blog-preview" key={blog.id}>
          <h2>{ blog.title }</h2>
          <p>Written by { blog.author }</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

## Destructuring Props

A more modern and cleaner approach is to use ES6 destructuring to pull properties directly out of the `props` object in the function's parameter list.

> [!TIP]
> Destructuring makes your component code more concise and readable by eliminating the need to repeatedly reference the `props` object.

```jsx
// Instead of (props), we use ({ blogs, title })
const BlogList = ({ blogs, title }) => {
  return (
    <div className="blog-list">
      {/* You can now use 'blogs' and 'title' variables directly */}
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      {blogs.map(blog => (
        <div className="blog-preview" key={blog.id}>
          <h2>{ blog.title }</h2>
          <p>Written by { blog.author }</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```
